  "strategy": "### 1. Approach and rationale\n\nThe strategy iteratively identifies and mitigates the worst-case overlap lags to minimize C\u2085. By adjusting h to reduce maximum overlap, it escapes local optima and approaches a better global solution. The approach is inspired by gradient-like methods but adapted for non-convexity and constraints.\n\n### 2. Mathematical specification\n\n- Variables: h \u2208 [0,1]^n, \u2211h = n/2, n=70\n- Objective: Minimize C\u2085 = max_k (h \u22c6 (1\u2212h))[k] * (2/n)\n- Cross-correlation overlap for lag k: sum_i h[i]*(1\u2212h[i+k])*dx\n- Operations: Identify max overlap lag, adjust h to reduce it, normalize\n\n### 3. Implementation procedure\n\n1. **Initialization**:\n    a. If initial_h exists, use it; else, random h with sum n/2\n    b. Compute initial C\u2085 and set \u03b5 = 0.01, max_iter\n\n2. **Main loop**:\n    a. Compute overlaps for all lags\n    b. Identify worst lag k\n    c. For each i:\n        i. Compute sensitivity\n        ii. Adjust h[i] -= \u03b4, h[i + k] += \u03b4\n        iii. Clip and normalize h\n    d. Update best solution if improved\n    e. Adjust \u03b5 based on progress\n\n3. **Termination**:\n    Return best h, C\u2085, and n_points\n\n### 4. Search choices\n\n- \u03b5 starts at 0.01, dynamically adjusted\n- Focus on top 1-5 worst lags per iteration\n\n### 5. Compute budget\n\n- Each iteration: O(n^2) for overlaps, O(n) adjustments\n- 600s allows ~1e4-1e5 steps\n\n### 6. Validation\n\n- Check h constraints, recompute C\u2085 for accuracy",
